'\" t
.TH NINIT.D 5 2026-09-30 "ninit @PACKAGE_VERSION@"
.SH NAME
ninit.d \- ninit service directory, service files and compiled graph
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IR @ninit_svcdir@/ name
.br
.IR @ninit_svcdir@/unused/ name
.br
.I @ninit_svcdir@/depgraph
.SH DESCRIPTION
each regular file in
.I @ninit_svcdir@
defines one service, named after the file.
symbolic links are followed.
.B ninitctl init
compiles the directory into
.IR depgraph ,
which is the only file
.BR ninit (8)
reads at boot.
a change to a service file takes effect after the graph is rebuilt and the system is rebooted
.PP
the following entries are skipped:
.IP \(bu 2
names beginning with a dot
.IP \(bu 2
directories and other files that are not regular
.IP \(bu 2
the reserved names
.BR unused ,
.BR depgraph ,
.B depgraph.old
and
.B depgraph.tmp
.IP \(bu 2
files that are themselves compiled graphs
.PP
a service name is 1 to 255 bytes and contains no slash, comma, whitespace or control character.
names ending in
.B ~
and names both beginning and ending in
.B #
are rejected.
an invalid name fails the build
.PP
.I unused/
holds disabled services and is not read.
.B ninitctl del
and
.B ninitctl add
move files into and out of it
.SH FILE FORMAT
a service file is a script for
.IR @ninit_shell@ .
its header is every line before the first line that is neither blank nor a comment.
a directive is a header line of the form
.PP
.RS
.BI #% key : \~value
.RE
.PP
whitespace around
.I key
and
.I value
is ignored.
keys are case sensitive.
.B depon
and
.B depof
accumulate when repeated.
any other repeated directive takes its last value.
an unknown key, a directive without a colon, or an invalid value fails the build
.PP
a
.B #%
line after the header is not a directive.
.B ninitctl init
warns about it, except inside a quoted string, a here-document or a file stored as written, and treats it as a comment
.PP
a
.B #!\&
line is an ordinary comment unless it names python, lua or perl, see
.BR "PYTHON, LUA AND PERL" .
otherwise the file is executed by the configured interpreter whatever its first line and mode.
.B ninitctl init
warns when the first line is a
.B #!\&
line naming any other interpreter, directly or through
.BR env .
.B #!/bin/sh
draws no warning when the interpreter lexes like sh
.PP
.B ninitctl init
stores the script without its comments, indentation or trailing blanks, and with runs of blanks collapsed to one space.
text inside quotes and here-documents is stored as written, and so is the rest of the file after a construct it cannot follow exactly, such as a line continuation inside a word.
a removed line stays as an empty line, so line numbers in interpreter messages match the file.
the stripper follows sh lexing and runs only when the interpreter is run as
.BR sh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR dash ,
.BR ash ,
.BR ksh ,
.BR mksh ,
.BR oksh ,
.BR loksh ,
.B yash
or
.BR posh .
for any other interpreter, such as zsh, the file is stored as written.
a file containing a NUL byte is rejected
.PP
a
.I duration
is a decimal integer followed by
.BR ms ,
.BR s ,
.B m
or
.BR h ,
or by nothing, meaning milliseconds.
it is at least 1 ms and at most 24 h.
a
.I list
is a sequence of service names separated by commas, whitespace or both
.SH DIRECTIVES
.TP
.BR type: " oneshot" | daemon | target
how the service becomes ready, see
.BR "SERVICE TYPES" .
the default is
.B oneshot
if the file contains a command and
.B target
if it does not
.TP
.BI depon: " list"
the services that must be up before this one is started
.TP
.BI depof: " list"
the services that must not start before this one is up.
.BI "depof: " y
in service
.I x
is equivalent to
.BI "depon: " x
in service
.I y
.TP
.BR deps: " uptime" | order
what the dependents of this service require of it, see
.BR DEPENDENCIES .
default
.B uptime
.TP
.BR onfail: " warn" | stop | shell
the action taken when the start attempts are exhausted, see
.BR FAILURE .
default
.B stop
if any service depends on this one, otherwise
.BR warn .
.B warn
on a service with dependents fails the build
.TP
.BI start-tries: " n"
the number of start attempts, 1 to 250.
default 1 for a oneshot and 2 for a daemon
.TP
.BI start-delay: " duration"
the delay between start attempts, at most 65535 ms.
a delay under 10 ms, including the default of none, is applied as 10 ms
.TP
.BI start-timeout: " duration"
the time allowed from spawn to ready.
the service is killed when it expires.
default 90 s
.TP
.BI stop-timeout: " duration"
the time allowed from
.B SIGTERM
to
.BR SIGKILL .
default 5 s
.TP
.BR restart: " always" | no
respawn the service when it exits after it has been ready, see
.BR RESTART .
.B never
is accepted for
.BR no .
daemons only.
default
.B no
.TP
.BI notify: " fd"
the descriptor, 3 to 255, on which the service reports readiness.
daemons only
.TP
.BI name: " name"
must equal the filename.
it has no other effect
.SH SERVICE TYPES
a service is ready when its dependents may start.
becoming ready makes it up
.TP
.B oneshot
ready when the script exits with status 0.
it remains up after it exits.
processes it leaves running remain in its cgroup
.TP
.B daemon
with
.BI notify: " n" ,
ready when data containing a newline is written to descriptor
.IR n ,
which holds the write end of a pipe.
data other than the newline is ignored.
without
.BR notify ,
ready once the interpreter has been executed.
up until its main process exits.
.B ninitctl init
warns about each daemon that has dependents and no
.B notify
.TP
.B target
ready when all of its prerequisites are up.
it has no commands and no process
.PP
a start attempt fails when a oneshot exits with a nonzero status or on a signal, when a daemon exits before it is ready, when every copy of a daemon's notify descriptor is closed before a newline, or when
.B start-timeout
expires
.PP
the main process of a daemon must remain in the foreground.
when it exits the service has exited, and its cgroup is killed with every process in it
.SH DEPENDENCIES
a service is started as soon as all of its prerequisites are up.
services with no prerequisites are started at boot.
a name that matches no service, a dependency on the service itself, and a cycle fail the build
.PP
.B deps
is set on the prerequisite:
.TP
.B uptime
dependents may start only while this service is up.
while it is down they are neither started nor restarted, and targets that depend on it are down.
if it exits after being ready and is not restarted, its dependents that have not started are skipped
.TP
.B order
dependents may start once this service has been ready once.
its later state does not affect them
.PP
running dependents are not stopped when a prerequisite goes down
.SH FAILURE
a failed start attempt kills the service's cgroup and process group.
while attempts remain, the service is started again after
.BR start-delay .
when all
.B start-tries
have failed the service is failed, the last 1024 bytes of its output are printed, and
.B onfail
applies:
.TP
.B warn
no further action
.TP
.B stop
every dependent that has not started is skipped.
other services are unaffected
.TP
.B shell
as
.BR stop ,
and the emergency shell is started
.PP
.B onfail
governs start attempts only.
.B ninitctl resume
returns failed and skipped services to pending
.SH RESTART
.B restart: always
applies once a daemon has been ready.
when it then exits, it goes down, its cgroup and process group are killed, and it is started again after 100, 250, 500, 1000 or 2000 ms for the first five consecutive exits and 5000 ms for each further one.
the count resets when the daemon has been up for 10 s.
a respawned daemon that fails to become ready is handled the same way, without limit.
a restart is deferred while a prerequisite with
.B deps: uptime
is down, and a service held by
.B ninitctl stop
is not restarted
.SH EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT
each start executes
.PP
.RS
.B @ninit_shell@ \-c
.I script name
.RE
.PP
where
.I script
is the file as stored by
.BR "ninitctl init" ,
without its comments, and
.I name
is the service name, available as
.BR $0 .
a python, lua or perl service is executed as in
.B "PYTHON, LUA AND PERL"
instead.
the process has:
.IP \(bu 2
a new session and no controlling terminal
.IP \(bu 2
the cgroup
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup/ninit.services/svc\- N ,
where
.I N
is the index of the service in the graph.
on linux 5.7 and later it is created inside the cgroup with
.BR CLONE_INTO_CGROUP ,
on older kernels it joins after
.BR fork (2)
.IP \(bu 2
standard input on
.IR /dev/null ,
standard output and standard error on a pipe read by
.B ninit
.IP \(bu 2
the notify pipe on its descriptor, and no other open descriptor
.IP \(bu 2
the environment
.BR PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin ,
.BR HOME=/ ,
.B TERM=linux
and the locale variables of
.IR /etc/locale.conf ,
or
.B LANG=C.UTF\-8
if it sets none
.IP \(bu 2
an empty signal mask and the default disposition for every signal
.IP \(bu 2
working directory
.I /
and umask 022
.IP \(bu 2
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
of 1024, hard limit 524288 or that of
.BR ninit ,
whichever is lower
.IP \(bu 2
.I oom_score_adj
0
.IP \(bu 2
user and group 0 and every capability
.PP
a signal sent to a service reaches every process in its cgroup and its process group.
.B SIGKILL
is delivered through
.IR cgroup.kill ,
and to the process group only on kernels before 5.14.
a service is stopped when its main process has been reaped and its cgroup is empty
.PP
.I /etc/locale.conf
contains
.IB name = value
lines for
.B LANG
and the
.B LC_*
variables.
a value may be quoted and consists of letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.BR . ,
.B \-
and
.BR @ .
lines beginning with
.B #
are comments and a later assignment overrides an earlier one
.SH PYTHON, LUA AND PERL
the configured interpreter is the language of service files.
python, lua and perl are an extra for the few services better written in one of them.
a file whose
.B #!\&
line names
.BR python ,
.BR python3 ,
.BR python3. N,
.BR lua ,
.BR lua N . N,
.B luajit
or a versioned
.BR luajit ,
.B perl
or
.BR perl N . N
runs under that interpreter.
it is named directly or through
.BR env .
.B ninitctl init
resolves it when the graph is built and stores its absolute path, ninit never runs
.BR env ,
so the options and
.IB name = value
words before the program, read as gnu and busybox
.B env
read them, have no effect.
.B env
is resolved through the
.B PATH
services run with, with the directory made canonical and the program keeping its own name.
a missing or non-executable interpreter fails the build.
the words after the interpreter are separate arguments, as
.B env \-S
splits them, and come before the script
.PP
the build fails on an argument that keeps the stored script from running.
a letter counts inside a cluster of options:
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
all|a word that is neither an option nor an option argument, \-, \-\-
python|\-c, \-m, \-h, \-V, \-\-help, \-\-version, and \-W, \-X or
\^|\-\-check\-hash\-based\-pycs without their argument
lua|\-e, luajit \-b, and \-l or luajit \-j without their argument
perl|\-e, \-E, \-x, \-c, \-h, \-u, \-v, \-V, \-d without a module,
\^|\-n, \-p, \-a, \-F, and \-I without its argument
.TE
.PP
the header, the
.B #!\&
line and directives included, is stored as empty lines and the rest of the file as written.
nothing is stripped and line numbers in interpreter messages match the file.
in lua a
.B #
line is valid only in the header.
each start executes, with
.I args
from the
.B #!\&
line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fIinterp\fR \-I \-B \-u \fIargs\fR \-c \fIscript name\fR
\fIinterp args\fR \-e \(aqio.stdout:setvbuf("line") arg = { [0] = "\fIname\fR" }\(aq \-e \fIscript\fR
\fIinterp args\fR \-e \(aq$| = 1; $0 = \(aq\(rs\(aq\(aq\fIname\fR\(aq\(rs\(aq\(aq;\(aq \-e \(aq#line 1\(aq \-e \fIscript\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
for python, lua and perl.
python sees the name in
.BR sys.argv[1] ,
lua in
.B arg[0]
and perl in
.BR $0 .
.B \-I
keeps
.IR / ,
the working directory, off
.B sys.path
and ignores the
.B PYTHON*
variables,
.B \-B
keeps python from writing
.I __pycache__
directories as root.
.BR \-u ,
the lua line buffering and perl's
.B $|
hand output to ninit as it is written.
the environment, descriptors, notify fd and readiness are those of
.BR "EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT" .
.PP
the syntax check of
.B ninitctl init
compiles python with
.B compile()
and lua with
.BR load() ,
neither runs the script.
.B perl \-c
runs
.B BEGIN
blocks and
.B use
statements as it compiles.
the interpreter must exist at its path when the service starts, a failed exec prints
.BI "ninit: exec " path ": errno " n
.SH OUTPUT
each line a service writes is logged as a
.B NOTE
line prefixed with the service name, see
.BR ninit (8).
lines longer than 256 bytes are split.
the last 1024 bytes are retained and printed when the service fails to start or exits after being ready
.SH DEPGRAPH
.I depgraph
is written by
.BR "ninitctl init" .
the previous graph is kept as
.IR depgraph.old .
.BR ninit (8)
loads a graph only if its version equals the one it was built with, currently 8,
and loads
.I depgraph.old
when
.I depgraph
fails.
the graph must be rebuilt after
.B ninit
is upgraded
.PP
all integers are unsigned and in host byte order.
sections are located by the offsets in the header, must lie within the file, and must not overlap
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lB lB lB
l n l.
section|align|size
header|8|64
service table|8|16 \(mu n_svc
dependent offsets|4|4 \(mu (n_svc + 1)
dependent indices|4|4 \(mu n_edges
policy table|4|16 \(mu n_svc
string table|1|blob_len
.TE
.PP
header:
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lB lB lB lB
l n l l.
offset|size|field|content
0x00|4|magic|0x4744494e, the bytes NIDG
0x04|4|version|8
0x08|4|crc32|crc32c of the file with this field 0
0x0c|4|total_len|file size
0x10|4|n_svc|services, at most 8192
0x14|4|n_roots|services without prerequisites
0x18|4|n_edges|dependency edges
0x1c|4|blob_len|string table size
0x20|4|off_svc|service table offset
0x24|4|off_rdep_off|dependent offsets offset
0x28|4|off_rdep_idx|dependent indices offset
0x2c|4|off_blob|string table offset
0x30|4|off_pol|policy table offset
0x34|4|reserved|0
0x38|8|srcs_hash|fnv-1a of the sources, not checked
.TE
.PP
service record:
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lB lB lB lB
l n l l.
offset|size|field|content
0x0|2|unmet|number of prerequisites
0x2|1|type|0 oneshot, 1 daemon, 2 target
0x3|1|flags|bits 1:0 onfail, bit 2 restart, bit 3 interpreter
0x4|2|n_desc|number of transitive dependents
0x6|2|notify_fd|0 or 3 to 255
0x8|4|script_off|string offset, 0xffffffff for a target
0xc|4|name_off|string offset
.TE
.PP
policy record:
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lB lB lB lB
l n l l.
offset|size|field|content
0x0|4|start_ms|start-timeout, 0 for 90000
0x4|4|stop_ms|stop-timeout, 0 for 5000
0x8|2|retry_ms|start-delay
0xa|1|start_tries|start-tries, 0 for the type default
0xb|1|pflags|bit 0 deps: order
0xc|4|exec_off|string offset of the interpreter argv, 0xffffffff without bit 3
.TE
.PP
the onfail field is 0 for warn, 1 for stop and 2 for shell.
services are in topological order, those without prerequisites first.
the dependents of service
.I i
are entries
.BI rdep_off[ i ]
up to but excluding
.BI rdep_off[ i +1]
of the dependent indices, ascending and each greater than
.IR i .
the string table holds NUL-terminated names and scripts.
an interpreter argv is two lists of NUL-terminated strings, each ended by an empty string.
the script is run by the first string of the first list, with the first list, the script and the second list as arguments.
the first string is an absolute path of at most 255 bytes, the lists hold at most 16 strings and each string is at most 4096 bytes.
unused bits and the reserved word are 0
.SH LIMITS
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
services|8192
name|255 bytes
script, without comments|131071 bytes
service file|135167 bytes
start-timeout, stop-timeout|24 h
start-delay|65535 ms
start-tries|250
notify|3 to 255
interpreter path|255 bytes
interpreter arguments|16 of 4096 bytes
retained output|1024 bytes
/etc/locale.conf|8191 bytes
.TE
.SH EXAMPLES
a mount step that the rest of the system depends on:
.PP
.EX
#!/bin/bash
#%depon: fs
#%onfail: shell
fsck \-A \-M \-T \-a \-t noopts=_netdev
(($? < 4)) || exit 1
exec mount \-a \-t nonfs,nfs4,cifs,smb3 \-O no_netdev
.EE
.PP
a daemon with a readiness probe.
the probe runs in a background subshell and writes the newline to descriptor 3.
the main shell closes its copy of descriptor 3 before it executes the daemon:
.PP
.EX
#!/bin/bash
#%depon: dbus, udev
#%type: daemon
#%notify: 3
#%restart: always
#%start\-timeout: 10s
{ gdbus wait \-\-system org.freedesktop.login1 && echo >&3; } &
exec 3>&\- /lib/elogind/elogind
.EE
.PP
a target:
.PP
.EX
#!/bin/bash
#%depon: mounts, tmpfiles, udev\-trigger, sysctl, hostname, console
.EE
.PP
a shell on the console, which must acquire the terminal itself:
.PP
.EX
#!/bin/bash
#%type: daemon
exec setsid \-c bash \-i < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
.EE
.PP
a complete service set, with disabled services in
.IR unused/ ,
is installed in
.IR @docdir@/ninit.d .
copy it to
.I @ninit_svcdir@
and move the services whose programs are not installed to
.I unused/
with
.BR "ninitctl del" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ninit (8),
.BR ninitctl (8),
.BR bash (1),
.BR cgroups (7)
